    "math_academy_id": "q-87613",
    "knowledge_point_id": "5a72dae6-2bbf-5e59-b71d-8ee57f589753",
    "knowledge_point": "Computing the Standard Deviation of a Data Set",
    "problem": "What is the standard deviation of the following data set?\n\n$$\n1,\\,1,\\,5,\\,4,\\,9\n$$",
    "worked_solution": "The standard deviation of a data set ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is the square root of the variance:\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\,\\,\\,\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-{(\\overset{―}{x})}^{2}}\n$$\n\nFirst, let's compute $\\overset{―}{x}.$ There are $n=5$ points in our data set, so the mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{x} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (1+1+5+4+9) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot 20 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}.$ Computing the mean of the squares of the points in the data set, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot ({1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{5}^{2}+{4}^{2}+{9}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (1+1+25+16+81) \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\overset{―}{{x}^{2}}-(\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{5}-{(4)}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{124}{5}-16 \\\\  & =\\frac{124-80}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{44}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the standard deviation is\n\n$$\n\\sigma =\\sqrt{{\\sigma }^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{44}{5}}.\n$$",
